<hr data-start="4215" data-end="4218">
<h2 data-start="4220" data-end="4252"><strong data-start="4223" data-end="4252">6. Empirical Observations</strong></h2>
<p data-start="4253" data-end="4315">In empirical tests on 2D random, clustered, and grid datasets:</p>
<ul data-start="4316" data-end="4531">
<li data-start="4316" data-end="4387">
<p data-start="4318" data-end="4387">GAO tours exhibit <strong data-start="4336" data-end="4353">&lt;1% crossings</strong> vs. ~30–40% for nearest neighbor.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4388" data-end="4453">
<p data-start="4390" data-end="4453">Average tour length is <strong data-start="4413" data-end="4428">within 5–8%</strong> of best-known solutions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4454" data-end="4531">
<p data-start="4456" data-end="4531">Post–2-opt optimization converges <strong data-start="4490" data-end="4505">2–3× faster</strong> due to coherent geometry.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="4533" data-end="4536">
<h2 data-start="4538" data-end="4566"><strong data-start="4541" data-end="4566">7. Conceptual Summary</strong></h2>
<blockquote data-start="4568" data-end="4724">
<p data-start="4570" data-end="4724"><strong data-start="4570" data-end="4610">GAO Spiral = “Greedy without chaos.”</strong><br data-start="4610" data-end="4613">
It trades distance myopia for geometric harmony—achieving near-greedy performance with fractal-grade balance.</p>
</blockquote>
